Two ways to print "Reminder 1", "Reminder 2", "Reminder 3":

**Copy-pasted:**
```
console.log("Reminder 1");
console.log("Reminder 2");
console.log("Reminder 3");
```

**Looped:**
```
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  console.log("Reminder", i + 1);
}
```

contentSections.body:
The most common loop shape in JavaScript is the **for loop**:

```
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  console.log("Reminder", i + 1);
}
```

It has three parts inside the parentheses, separated by semicolons: `let i = 0` (start a counter at 0), `i < 3` (keep going while this is true), `i++` (add 1 to `i` after each pass). The block in `{ }` — the **loop body** — runs once per pass, using whatever `i` currently is. The next lesson breaks each of the three parts down in detail; for now, the shape to recognize is: start somewhere, keep going while a condition holds, do something each time, move the counter.

